`merge_intervals(intervals)` takes a list of `[start, end]` pairs (start <= end) and returns the merged intervals as `[start, end]` lists sorted by start. Intervals that overlap or touch (one ends where the next starts) merge.

The implementation below has a bug: it fails at least one of the tests shown. Fix it so every test passes. The fixed function is also run against held-out tests of the same behavior, so fix the bug itself rather than special-casing these tests.

```python
def merge_intervals(intervals):
    merged = []
    for start, end in sorted(intervals):
        if merged and start <= merged[-1][1]:
            merged[+1][1] = max(merged[-1][1], end)
        else:
            merged.append([start, end])
    return merged
```

Tests:

```python
assert merge_intervals([[1, 3], [2, 6], [8, 10]]) == [[1, 6], [8, 10]]
assert merge_intervals([[5, 7], [1, 2]]) == [[1, 2], [5, 7]]
assert merge_intervals([[1, 4], [4, 5]]) == [[1, 5]]
assert merge_intervals([[1, 10], [2, 3]]) == [[1, 10]]
assert merge_intervals([]) == []
